  "readmeFile": "ReadMe.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Version 7.0.421\n\nFix: AmsTcpIpRouter no longer terminates the process when handling a received frame fails.\n\nVersion 7.0.403\n\nEnh: The AmsTcpIpRouter now sends AMS router notifications to connected loopback peers on start and stop, so RouterStateChanged also works with RouterType.CustomRouter, #337370\n\nVersion 7.0.339\n\nFix: AmsTcpIpRouter no longer terminates when a client closes an AMS port of a previous router instance, #330629\n\nVersion 7.0.335\n\nFix: The managed AMS/TCP router no longer opens a second connection when local clients connect to several AMS ports of the same target at once (e.g. 851 and 852), and reconnects a connection closed by the remote system instead of failing with TargetMachineNotFound (e.g. after a router restart), #330202\n\nVersion 7.0.314\n\nFix: Since 7.0.147 the managed AMS/TCP router opened a new TCP connection for every request to targets whose AmsNetId doesn't end in .1.1 (e.g. a.b.c.d.2.1), causing ClientSyncTimeOut from the second request on, #323440\n\nVersion 7.0.286\n\nFix: The managed AMS/TCP router rejects AMS/TCP frames larger than 64 MiB and closes the connection (denial-of-service hardening).\n\nVersion 7.0.60\n\nEnh: AmsRouter not using app local UnixSocket path as fallback anymore.\n\nEnh: Using LoopbackConnection as fallback, when no UnixSocket path is specified by configuration.\n\nVersion 7.0.36\n\nBreakingChange: Removed the net6.0 target (.NET 6 is out of support)\n\nVersion 7.0\n\nEnh: Supports internal communication via UnixSockets or TCP/IP Loopback\n\nEnh: TargetFramework dependencies changed to net8.0, net6.0, netstandard2.0",
